import{_ as t,o,c as r,a2 as d}from"./chunks/framework.BM5YPQmL.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"The merged intent apps (WP-l1apps-merge)","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"l1/merged-apps.md","filePath":"l1/merged-apps.md"}'),a={name:"l1/merged-apps.md"};function i(s,e,c,n,l,p){return o(),r("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[d('<h1 id="the-merged-intent-apps-wp-l1apps-merge" tabindex="-1">The merged intent apps (WP-l1apps-merge) <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-merged-intent-apps-wp-l1apps-merge" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The merged intent apps (WP-l1apps-merge)&quot;">​</a></h1><p>WP-l1apps-merge replaced the &quot;one wrapper app per L1SVC op&quot; sprawl with eight intent-driven applications. A user no longer memorizes 200+ command spellings: they run one app, pick a numbered choice from a menu, and the wizard drives the underlying L0 services for them - and when a step cannot proceed, the wizard offers the automatic repair path (DHCP when no IP, A/B init when the disk is absent, ...).</p><p>Every classic spelling keeps working (zero feature loss): the pre-merge one-command wrappers live on as <code>direct &lt;word&gt; ...</code> (explicit), bare word passthrough (geek mode), and as their own independent programs where they are not service ops (ls, cp, ...).</p><h2 id="the-eight-apps" tabindex="-1">The eight apps <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-eight-apps" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The eight apps&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>App</th><th>Menu domain</th><th>Wizard repairs</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>update</code></td><td>OTA: network / A/B disk / <strong>update source (mirror)</strong> / check / install</td><td>DHCP, A/B init</td></tr><tr><td><code>disk-tool</code></td><td>block devices, partitions, mkfs, mounts, fsck, stats, A/B mgmt</td><td>re-confirm by retyping the device</td></tr><tr><td><code>net-tool</code></td><td>IP, DHCP, static IP, DNS, WiFi, Bluetooth, firewall, routes, tests</td><td>DHCP</td></tr><tr><td><code>sound-tool</code></td><td>card detection, volume, playback, per-driver status, self-tests</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td><code>files</code></td><td>ls / tree / df+du / cp / mv / rm / edit / stat+chmod as real child processes</td><td>fork + exec + wait</td></tr><tr><td><code>sysdiag</code></td><td>memory, heap, frag, scheduler, health, dmesg, logcat, crashlog, memtest</td><td>confirm before memtest</td></tr><tr><td><code>hwinfo</code></td><td>PCI, PCIe, disks, USB (+ per-slot detail), input, I2C/SMBus</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td><code>power</code></td><td>power/ACPI status, watchdog, <strong>reboot / shutdown / halt (each with a bare-y confirm)</strong></td><td>-</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="how-a-wizard-drives-a-service" tabindex="-1">How a wizard drives a service <a class="header-anchor" href="#how-a-wizard-drives-a-service" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;How a wizard drives a service&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The bridge is one syscall: <code>SYS_L1SVC(cat, op, arg, out, cap)</code> (110). <code>ocos/interface/l1svc_ops.h</code> is the generated op-number mirror; the apps include it and pick constants - no registration, no new build rules (a folder with <code>main.c</code> under <code>ocos/l1apps/&lt;name&gt;/</code> plus <code>i18n/{en,zh}.json</code> is discovered automatically by <code>tools/gen_userprogs.py</code>).</p><p><code>ocos/interface/wizard_lib.h</code> is the shared toolkit (header-only, every app compiles its own copy):</p><ul><li><code>wiz_svc(cat, op, arg)</code> runs one op and prints the captured text verbatim; <code>wiz_svc_quiet(..., out, cap)</code> captures without printing.</li><li><code>wiz_find(hay, needle)</code> probes the FIXED ANCHOR substrings the L0 services render (an English-text contract, stable across releases): <code>A/B disk: present</code>, <code>IP: 0.0.0.0</code>, <code>New version available.</code>, <code>Current version is up to date.</code></li><li><code>wiz_token_after(...)</code> lifts a value (<code>IP: 10.0.2.15</code> -&gt; the address).</li><li><code>wiz_ask</code> / <code>wiz_confirm</code> / <code>wiz_pause</code> / <code>wiz_menu</code> - the input layer. All waits block on the kernel line reader (SYS_READLINE, hlt-sleeps): a menu choice is <code>&lt;digit&gt;&lt;ENTER&gt;</code> (or <code>q</code>), a confirm is only a bare <code>y</code>. A busy ring-3 poll of the raw-key queue was tried and rejected - millions of int-0x80 traps per second starve the kernel tick work (net_poll / tcp_check_rto) on a single CPU, and the system stalls with the poller inside it; see KNOWN_ISSUES.</li></ul><h2 id="the-update-wizard-s-mirror-step" tabindex="-1">The update wizard&#39;s mirror step <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-update-wizard-s-mirror-step" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The update wizard&#39;s mirror step&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Step 3 of <code>update</code> shows the active source (<code>config get update_url</code>) and offers the documented mirrors:</p><ol><li>official - <code>https://cubestudio-dev.github.io/OpenCubeOS/update.json</code></li><li>GitHub raw mirror - <code>https://raw.githubusercontent.com/cubestudio-dev/OpenCubeOS/gh-pages/update.json</code></li><li>jsDelivr CDN mirror - <code>https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/cubestudio-dev/OpenCubeOS@gh-pages/update.json</code></li><li>custom URL (validated to start with <code>http://</code> or <code>https://</code>)</li></ol><p>The pick is persisted with <code>config set update_url &lt;url&gt;</code> - the same mechanism a hand edit would use, nothing magic - and the host inside the active URL is resolved over DNS before the check runs. Large packages never stress memory: the service-side downloader (<code>ota_update_download</code>) streams to disk in 4 KiB chunks (DL_CHUNK), so the update works for files far larger than RAM.</p><h2 id="why-the-i18n-files-matter" tabindex="-1">Why the i18n files matter <a class="header-anchor" href="#why-the-i18n-files-matter" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Why the i18n files matter&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Every app carries <code>i18n/{en,zh}.json</code>; <code>tr_(&quot;key&quot;)</code> renders UI text and a miss falls back to the key itself, so an incomplete resource can never crash a wizard. The four common keys every merged app ships: <code>wiz_quit</code>, <code>wiz_select</code>, <code>wiz_pause</code>, <code>wiz_svc_failed</code>. Service output is never translated - the English anchors are the contract the wizards parse.</p><h2 id="verification" tabindex="-1">Verification <a class="header-anchor" href="#verification" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Verification&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><code>tools/wp_wizard_test.py</code> - the <code>update</code> state machine end to end under QEMU (network step, A/B guidance, line-reader echo, mismatch abort, 6/6).</li><li><code>tools/wp_smoke_test.py</code> - every app answers its menu (<code>q</code> quits) and its passthrough words; 11/11.</li><li>Full-closure evidence: a live QEMU run walked DHCP -&gt; abdisk init -&gt; mirror saved to /etc/opencube.conf -&gt; DNS 185.185.109.153 -&gt; <code>Current version is up to date.</code> against the real GitHub Pages manifest.</li></ul><p>Known limitation while testing: attaching a second disk to this QEMU build (IDE or virtio) starves the guest with kernel tick work - the wizard itself stays correct (state machine test runs without the extra disk); the phenomenon is recorded in KNOWN_ISSUES.md.</p>',18)])])}const m=t(a,[["render",i]]);export{u as __pageData,m as default};
